"""Chapter 1 drill items — the study of government, the state, forms, ideologies, sovereignty."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_1 = [
    q("Government is best defined as the", ["group of people who rule a state at a particular time", "institution through which a state is ruled", "territory over which a state exercises control", "body of citizens who elect their rulers"], 1, "The institution through which a state is ruled. The group in office is only one sense of the word, territory is an element of the state rather than government, and the electorate is the source of authority, not the machinery."),
    q("Which of the following is an element of the state?", ["Constitution", "Sovereignty", "Army", "Flag"], 1, "Sovereignty is one of the four elements. A constitution and an army are parts of the machinery of government, and a flag is a symbol of the state rather than a condition of it."),
    q("The four elements of the state are population, territory, government and", ["constitution", "recognition", "sovereignty", "citizenship"], 2, "Sovereignty completes the four. Recognition by other states is evidence that the elements exist, and a constitution is part of the machinery of government."),
    q("A nation differs from a state in that a nation", ["has a defined territory", "is a people sharing a common history and culture", "exercises sovereignty over its affairs", "must be recognised by the United Nations"], 1, "A nation is a people bound by history, culture or language. Territory, sovereignty and recognition are marks of the state, which may contain many nations."),
    q("Nigeria is best described as", ["a nation-state", "a state of many nations", "a confederation of nations", "a single nation with one culture"], 1, "A state of many nations: over two hundred and fifty ethnic groups live within one sovereign state. A nation-state is the opposite case, where one nation roughly fills the state."),
    q("Society differs from the state because society", ["is older and wider than the state", "has a defined territory", "exercises sovereignty", "is created by the constitution"], 0, "Society is the whole web of relationships — families, markets, churches, unions — and it existed before the state and extends beyond it. Territory and sovereignty belong to the state."),
    q("A change of government in a country does not necessarily mean that", ["the constitution has changed", "the state has changed", "the ruling party has changed", "the president has changed"], 1, "The state persists through changes of government. The constitution, the ruling party and the officeholders can all change while the state remains the same legal and territorial unit."),
    q("Sovereignty is best defined as", ["the power to make laws", "supreme authority within a territory and independence from external control", "the right to vote in elections", "the power to tax citizens"], 1, "Sovereignty is supreme internal authority plus external independence. Law-making and taxation are powers a sovereign authority may exercise, not sovereignty itself."),
    q("In a federation, sovereignty is", ["shared between the federal and state governments", "exercised only by the federal government", "indivisible, although governmental powers are divided", "held by the constituent states"], 2, "Sovereignty is indivisible; what a federation divides is the exercise of governmental powers between levels. The option that says sovereignty is shared is the standard trap."),
    q("The doctrine that all political authority comes from the people is called", ["legal sovereignty", "popular sovereignty", "external sovereignty", "parliamentary sovereignty"], 1, "Popular sovereignty. Section 14 of the 1999 Constitution states that sovereignty belongs to the people, from whom government derives its authority."),
    q("A government that is lawful but whose right to rule is widely rejected lacks", ["sovereignty", "legitimacy", "territory", "a constitution"], 1, "Legitimacy is the acceptance by the governed that a government has the right to rule. A rigged election can produce a legal government that nobody accepts."),
    q("The mandate of a government is", ["its legal right to exist", "the authority it claims from its election victory", "the oath taken by its officials", "the budget it presents to parliament"], 1, "A mandate is the claim that the voters endorsed the programme on which the government fought the election. Legality is a different question, and an oath and a budget are instruments rather than sources of authority."),
    q("Which of the following is a feature of democracy?", ["Rule by a hereditary monarch", "Periodic free and fair elections", "Rule by the military", "Government by a single party"], 1, "Free and fair elections are the defining feature, along with majority rule, minority rights and the rule of law. Hereditary rule, military rule and one-party rule are each its opposite."),
    q("A monarchy in which the monarch reigns but does not rule is called", ["an absolute monarchy", "a constitutional monarchy", "an oligarchy", "a republic"], 1, "A constitutional monarchy. In an absolute monarchy the monarch governs; in a republic there is no monarch at all."),
    q("Government by a small group of people is called", ["democracy", "oligarchy", "monarchy", "theocracy"], 1, "Oligarchy is rule by the few — the wealthy, the military or a clan. Aristocracy is its “rule by the best” version, and theocracy is rule by religious authority."),
    q("Which of the following best describes a dictatorship?", ["Government by an elected assembly", "Rule by one person or party without effective constitutional limits", "Rule by a hereditary ruler under a constitution", "Government by judges"], 1, "A dictatorship is unrestrained power in one person or party. An elected assembly, a constitutional monarch and a judiciary are all limits of one kind or another."),
    q("The main difference between a republic and a monarchy is that in a republic", ["there are no elections", "the head of state is not a hereditary monarch", "the rule of law does not apply", "the people have no rights"], 1, "A republic is defined by how the head of state is chosen, not by how free the country is. A republic can be democratic or dictatorial, and a monarchy can be either."),
    q("A system in which political authority is exercised by religious leaders and law is derived from a sacred text is called", ["a theocracy", "an aristocracy", "a plutocracy", "a bureaucracy"], 0, "Theocracy. Aristocracy is rule by a supposed elite, plutocracy by the wealthy, and bureaucracy by officials."),
    q("Capitalism is an economic system in which", ["the state owns the major means of production", "the means of production are privately owned and profit is the motive", "all property is held in common", "land is the only source of wealth"], 1, "Private ownership and the profit motive define capitalism. Public ownership describes socialism, common ownership describes communism, and land as the source of status describes feudalism."),
    q("Socialism is best distinguished from communism by the fact that socialism", ["abolishes private property entirely", "allows some private ownership and does not require the abolition of class", "is a system of hereditary rule", "rejects all forms of planning"], 1, "Socialism socialises the key means of production but leaves room for small private business and does not claim to have abolished class. Communism is the Marxist end-point in which private property and class disappear."),
    q("In which system is land the principal source of wealth, status and power?", ["Capitalism", "Feudalism", "Socialism", "Welfarism"], 1, "Feudalism. Land is held by a lord in return for service and worked by a tied peasantry, and the economy is agricultural rather than industrial."),
    q("Fascism is characterised by", ["the supremacy of the individual over the state", "the supremacy of the state over the individual", "the abolition of the state", "rule by a religious authority"], 1, "Fascism exalts the nation and the state above the individual, under one leader and one party. It is totalitarian in politics even where it leaves private property intact."),
    q("A system that keeps private ownership of the means of production but provides health care, education and a minimum income through the state is called", ["socialism", "welfarism", "communism", "mercantilism"], 1, "Welfarism — capitalism with a safety net. The dividing line between it and socialism is who owns the major means of production, not who pays for health care."),
    q("The Nigerian economy is best described as", ["purely capitalist", "purely socialist", "mixed, with private ownership and state participation and regulation", "feudal"], 2, "A mixed economy: private firms own most production, the state owns oil and infrastructure and regulates the rest, and the Constitution protects private property while setting social objectives."),
    q("The rule of law, as stated by Dicey, includes all of the following except", ["supremacy of the law", "equality before the law", "the constitution as the result of the ordinary law", "the supremacy of the executive over the courts"], 3, "The supremacy of the executive over the courts is the negation of the rule of law. The other three are Dicey's three limbs."),
    q("The principle that a person may not be punished except for a breach of the law established in the ordinary courts is part of the", ["separation of powers", "rule of law", "doctrine of sovereignty", "federal principle"], 1, "That is the first limb of the rule of law. Separation of powers concerns the division of functions, sovereignty concerns supreme authority, and federalism concerns the division of power between levels."),
    q("The doctrine of separation of powers is usually associated with", ["Dicey", "Montesquieu", "Hobbes", "Rousseau"], 1, "Montesquieu, in The Spirit of the Laws. Dicey is associated with the rule of law, Hobbes with absolute sovereignty and Rousseau with the general will."),
    q("Separation of powers is intended chiefly to", ["speed up legislation", "prevent the concentration of power in one organ", "increase the power of the executive", "reduce the cost of government"], 1, "The doctrine exists to prevent tyranny by keeping the three functions in different hands. It may slow government down, which is the price it pays for safety."),
    q("A constitution is best defined as", ["a law passed by the legislature", "the body of fundamental rules by which a state is governed", "the manifesto of the ruling party", "a treaty between states"], 1, "The body of fundamental rules, written or unwritten, creating the organs of government and limiting their powers. A statute, a manifesto and a treaty are none of these things."),
    q("Constitutionalism means", ["the existence of a written constitution", "the practice of governing within the rules of the constitution", "the amendment of the constitution", "the suspension of the constitution"], 1, "Constitutionalism is the practice of being bound by the rules. A state can have a constitution without constitutionalism — Nigeria under military rule is the standard example."),
    q("A constitution contained in a single document is said to be", ["unwritten", "written", "flexible", "customary"], 1, "Written. Whether it is easy or hard to amend is a separate question, which is why written does not imply rigid."),
    q("The constitution of the United Kingdom is usually described as", ["written and rigid", "unwritten and flexible", "written and flexible", "unwritten and rigid"], 1, "Unwritten and flexible: it is found in statutes, conventions and cases, and Parliament can change it by ordinary legislation."),
    q("A constitution that can be amended only by a special majority or a referendum is", ["flexible", "rigid", "unwritten", "customary"], 1, "Rigid. The test is the amending procedure, not whether the rules are written down."),
    q("The main advantage of a rigid constitution is that it", ["can be changed quickly in a crisis", "protects rights against a temporary majority", "does not need a legislature", "is easier to understand"], 1, "A rigid constitution entrenches rights and institutions against a passing majority. Its disadvantage is that it also entrenches mistakes."),
    q("Government is studied mainly in order to understand", ["the history of political parties", "power, order, the citizen and political change", "the economy of a country", "the laws of other countries"], 1, "The four standard answers are power, order, the citizen and change. Party history, economics and foreign law are each a part of the subject rather than its purpose."),
    q("A state in which the borders of the state and the boundaries of a single nation roughly coincide is called", ["a nation-state", "a federation", "a confederation", "an empire"], 0, "A nation-state, such as Japan or Iceland. Nigeria is the opposite case: one state, many nations."),
    q("External sovereignty means", ["the power to make treaties", "independence from the control of other states", "the power to tax imports", "the right to send ambassadors"], 1, "External sovereignty is independence: no other state may command the state. Treaties, tariffs and embassies are powers it exercises, not the sovereignty itself."),
    q("Which of the following would most clearly show that a government has lost legitimacy?", ["It loses a vote on a minor bill", "A general strike and mass protests reject its authority", "It reshuffles the cabinet", "It loses a court case"], 1, "Mass rejection of its authority is the test of legitimacy. Losing a vote, a reshuffle and a court defeat are ordinary events in a legitimate system."),
    q("A government elected with 30 per cent of the vote but 60 per cent of the seats may claim", ["sovereignty", "a mandate", "judicial review", "federal character"], 1, "It may claim a mandate from the election result, however thin the popular vote. Sovereignty belongs to the people, judicial review belongs to the courts, and federal character is a principle of appointment."),
    q("Which of the following is NOT a form of government?", ["Democracy", "Oligarchy", "Sovereignty", "Monarchy"], 2, "Sovereignty is an element of the state, not a form of government. Democracy, oligarchy and monarchy each answer the question of who rules."),
]
